Title: The Pine Woods
Order: 1
Production Code: 1x01

Radio play, Part 1 of 6. Adapts Part 1 of the novella. Running time about 11 minutes.

INT. SOUND STAGE - BLACK

AMB: absolute quiet, a room tone just barely there

MUSIC: BED IN - a single sustained low string, no melody, like a held breath

[[The Narrator]] speaks close to the microphone, dry, measured, in the cadence of a nineteenth-century lecture hall.

NARRATOR
(quiet, unhurried)
The State of Virginia is famous in American annals for the multitudinous array of her statesmen and heroes. She has been dignified by some the mother of statesmen.

SFX: a page turning, very near

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Yet not all the great ones of the Old Dominion have, by the fact of their birth-place, escaped undeserved obscurity. By some strange neglect, one of the truest, manliest, and bravest of her children holds now no higher place in the records of that grand old Commonwealth than is held by a horse or an ox.

MUSIC: STING - one low note, then nothing

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
(a little harder)
A man who loved liberty as well as did Patrick Henry, who deserved it as much as Thomas Jefferson, and who fought for it with a valor as high, an arm as strong, and against odds as great, as he who led all the armies of the American colonies through the great war for freedom and independence, lives now only in the chattel records of his native State.

AMB: wind begins, far off, through high branches

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
(lower)
Glimpses of this great character are all that can now be presented. Like a guiding star on a stormy night, he is seen through the parted clouds and the howling tempests; or, like the gray peak of a menacing rock on a perilous coast, he is seen by the quivering flash of angry lightning, and he again disappears covered with mystery.

SFX: distant thunder, one long roll, fading

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Speaking of marks, traces, possibles, and probabilities, we come before our listeners.

MUSIC: BED OUT - the string note thins to nothing as the wind rises

EXT. THE PINE FOREST - A BROOK - MORNING

AMB: a brook running over stones, wind in pine boughs, birdsong beginning

SFX: a horse's slow hooves on soft earth, a bridle jingle, the horse drinking

SFX: church bells, very far away, three slow peals

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the spring of 1835, on a Sabbath morning, within hearing of the solemn peals of the church bells at a distant village, a Northern traveller through the State of Virginia drew up his horse to drink at a sparkling brook, near the edge of a dark pine forest.

SFX: the horse stops drinking, lifts its head

[[Mr. Listwell]] stands very still, listening. A human voice, deep and far inside the trees.

LISTWELL
(under his breath)
To whom can he be speaking? He seems to be alone.

SFX: boots on pine needles, careful, a rope tied to a branch, the horse blowing

SFX: a branch cracks, and he freezes

AMB: the pines, nothing but the pines, a bird calling twice

SFX: the soft rustle of a man lowering himself behind a huge fallen log

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Tieing his horse at a short distance from the brook, he stealthily drew near the solitary speaker; and, concealing himself by the side of a huge fallen tree, he distinctly heard the following soliloquy.

EXT. THE PINE FOREST - A CLEARING - CONTINUOUS

AMB: closer pines, the brook now a murmur, birds in the high boughs

MUSIC: BED IN - a low, patient cello, hovering on one note

[[Madison Washington]] is standing alone among the trees. We hear him before we ever see him: a full, melodious voice, grave and rough with feeling.

MADISON
(to himself, low)
What, then, is life to me? It is aimless and worthless, and worse than worthless.

SFX: wings overhead, small birds startled up from a bough

MADISON (CONT'D)
(watching the birds, bitter)
Those birds, perched on yon swinging boughs, in friendly conclave, sounding forth their merry notes in seeming worship of the rising sun, though liable to the sportsman's fowling-piece, are still my superiors. They live free, though they may die slaves.

AMB: the birds again, bright, unbothered

MADISON (CONT'D)
But what is freedom to me, or I to it? I am a slave. Born a slave, an abject slave. Even before I made part of this breathing world, the scourge was platted for my back; the fetters were forged for my limbs.

SFX: dry leaves, something long gliding away through them

MADISON (CONT'D)
(seeing it)
How mean a thing am I. That accursed and crawling snake, that miserable reptile, that has just glided into its slimy home, is freer and better off than I. He escaped my blow, and is safe.

SFX: a stick struck against a tree trunk, once, hard

MADISON (CONT'D)
(ashamed, rising)
But here am I, a man. Yes, a man! With thoughts and wishes, with powers and faculties as far as angel's flight above that hated reptile, and yet he is my superior, and scorns to own me as his master, or to stop to take my blows. When he saw my uplifted arm, he darted beyond my reach, and turned to give me battle. I dare not do as much as that. I neither run nor fight, but do meanly stand, answering each heavy blow of a cruel master with doleful wails and piteous cries.

MUSIC: BED OUT - the cello drops away

MADISON (CONT'D)
(a breath)
I am galled with irons; but even these are more tolerable than the consciousness, the galling consciousness of cowardice and indecision.

AMB: wind in the pines, a long gust

MADISON (CONT'D)
(suddenly)
Can it be that I dare not run away? Perish the thought. I dare do anything which may be done by another.

SFX: water, a splash and a struggle remembered, then gone

MADISON (CONT'D)
(remembering)
When that young man struggled with the waves for life, and others stood back appalled in helpless horror, did I not plunge in, forgetful of life, to save his?

SFX: a bull bellowing, far off, then the thud of hooves, then silence

MADISON (CONT'D)
The raging bull from whom all others fled, pale with fright, did I not keep at bay with a single pitchfork? Could a coward do that? No, no. I wrong myself. I am no coward.

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, rising slowly, a pulse under them

MADISON (CONT'D)
(firm, each phrase a decision)
Liberty I will have, or die in the attempt to gain it. This working that others may live in idleness! This cringing submission to insolence and curses! This living under the constant dread and apprehension of being sold and transferred, like a mere brute, is too much for me. I will stand it no longer.

AMB: the pines, listening

MADISON (CONT'D)
What others have done, I will do. These trusty legs, or these sinewy arms, shall place me among the free. Tom escaped; so can I. The North Star will not be less kind to me than to him. I will follow it. I will at least make the trial.

SFX: the cow-skin remembered, a whip across the air, too short to be real

MADISON (CONT'D)
(a short bitter laugh)
I have nothing to lose. If I am caught, I shall only be a slave. If I am shot, I shall only lose a life which is a burden and a curse. If I get clear, as something tells me I shall, liberty, the inalienable birthright of every man, precious and priceless, will be mine.

MUSIC: STING - one clear, rising note

MADISON (CONT'D)
(quietly, absolute)
My resolution is fixed. I shall be free.

AMB: the wind lifts and the birds answer, all at once

EXT. THE PINE FOREST - BEHIND THE FALLEN LOG - CONTINUOUS

AMB: pines, close, the held breath of a hidden man

SFX: Listwell's breath, in and out, trying not to be heard

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At these words the traveller raised his head cautiously and noiselessly, and caught, from his hiding-place, a full view of the unsuspecting speaker.

MUSIC: BED OUT - strings settle

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Madison was of manly form. Tall, symmetrical, round, and strong. In his movements he seemed to combine, with the strength of the lion, a lion's elasticity. His torn sleeves disclosed arms like polished iron. His face was "black, but comely." A child might play in his arms, or dance on his shoulders. A giant's strength, but not a giant's heart was in him. His voice, that unfailing index of the soul, though full and melodious, had that in it which could terrify as well as charm.

SFX: Madison's breathing, steadier now, the sound of a man who has made up his mind

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
He was one to be sought as a friend, but to be dreaded as an enemy.

EXT. THE PINE FOREST - A CLEARING - MOMENTS LATER

AMB: pines, wind, the brook far behind

Madison is speaking again, but not to the sky now. He is speaking, painfully, to someone who isn't there.

MADISON
(lower, breaking)
How can I leave her? Poor thing. What can she do when I am gone? Oh. Oh. 'Tis impossible that I can leave poor Susan.

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - the church bells' last peal, as if from another country

[[Susan]] is never heard in this scene. That is the point: she is a voice Madison carries.

MADISON (CONT'D)
(arguing it through, to himself)
All is uncertain here. To-morrow's sun may not rise before I am sold, and separated from her I love. What, then, could I do for her? I should be in more hopeless slavery, and she no nearer to liberty. Whereas if I were free, my arms my own, I might devise the means to rescue her.

SFX: a sudden stillness, birds quiet, the pines holding still

Madison stops. He listens, as if the thought of being overheard has just flashed across his mind. We hear him listening: the quiet of a man who is waiting for the woods to answer.

SFX: Madison's footsteps, measured, moving away through the pine needles

AMB: wind returns, a long gust, then the brook again

MUSIC: SOURCE BED OUT

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He said no more, but, with measured steps, walked away, and was lost to the eye of our traveller amidst the wildering woods.

EXT. THE PINE FOREST - BEHIND THE FALLEN LOG - LATER

AMB: the pines, hushed

SFX: Listwell rising slowly, his knees cracking, a man who has been still for a long time

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Long after Madison had left the ground, Mr. Listwell remained in motionless silence, meditating on the extraordinary revelations to which he had listened.

LISTWELL
(half to himself, shaken)
Here is indeed a man of rare endowments. A child of God, guilty of no crime but the color of his skin, hiding away from the face of humanity, and pouring out his thoughts and feelings, his hopes and resolutions, to the lonely woods.

SFX: church bells again, very distant, a single peal

LISTWELL (CONT'D)
(bitter)
To him those distant church bells have no grateful music. He shuns the church, the altar, and the great congregation of christian worshippers, and wanders away to the gloomy forest, to utter in the vacant air complaints and griefs which the religion of his times and his country can neither console nor relieve.

AMB: the wind through the pines, steady, like breath

LISTWELL (CONT'D)
(a decision, not a speech)
From this hour I am an abolitionist. I have seen enough and heard enough, and I shall go to my home in Ohio resolved to atone for my past indifference to this ill-starred race.

MUSIC: BED IN - a low strings and the cello together, warm for the first time

LISTWELL (CONT'D)
By making such exertions as I shall be able to do, for the speedy emancipation of every slave in the land.

SFX: the horse whickers as he approaches, and his hand on its neck

SFX: a saddle creaking, a rider mounting, hooves moving off through pine needles

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ohio. He had no way of knowing that he would see that face again.

AMB: the wind, now with a hint of something far larger behind it, like an ocean heard through a wall

MUSIC: BED OUT - slowly, to the single held string note

NARRATOR
(quiet)
Part Two. The Fireside.

MUSIC: STING - the string note, cut off

AMB: silence
